Definition — (d) Risk-sensitive constraints: CVaR For an integrable cost $Z$ ($\mathbb E|Z|<\infty$; large is bad) and $\alpha\in(0,1)$, the conditional value-at-risk in the Rockafellar–Uryasev form used by Chow, Ghavamzadeh, Janson & Pavone, JMLR 2018 is $$\operatorname{CVaR}_\alpha(Z)=\min_{
u\in\mathbb R}\Big\{
u+ rac{1}{1-\alpha}\,\mathbb E ig[(Z-
u)^+ ig]\Big\},$$ the average of the worst $(1-\alpha)$-fraction of outcomes: $\alpha	o1$ is the tail, $\alpha	o0$ recovers $\mathbb E[Z]$. A risk constraint reads $\operatorname{CVaR}_\alpha(\sum_t c_t)\le d$. Example: the 5% worst episodes must average fewer than $d$ contacts. It controls frequency and magnitude of bad episodes, which neither (b) nor (c) does alone (first walkthrough below, Step 5).

Derivation — Lie derivatives: why the barrier condition is affine in $u$Here $x\in\mathbb R^n$, $u\in\mathbb R^m$, $f(x)\in\mathbb R^n$, $g(x)\in\mathbb R^{n	imes m}$ and $h(x)\in\mathbb R$. Along a trajectory the chain rule gives$$\dot h= rac{d}{dt}h(x(t))=
abla h(x)^{	op}\dot x=\underbrace{
abla h(x)^{	op}f(x)}_{L_fh(x)}+\underbrace{
abla h(x)^{	op}g(x)}_{L_gh(x)}\,u ,$$with the Lie derivatives $L_fh(x)$ (a scalar) and $L_gh(x)$ (a $1	imes m$ row vector). At each fixed $x$ the barrier inequality $L_fh(x)+L_gh(x)u+\alpha(h(x))\ge0$ is therefore one linear inequality in $u$, which is why the CBF-QP has linear constraints. ($C^1$ means that the first partial derivatives exist and are continuous.)

